+name: Claude Code Review
+
+on:
+  pull_request:
+    types: [opened]
+  issue_comment:
+    types: [created]
+  pull_request_review:
+    types: [submitted]
+  pull_request_review_comment:
+    types: [created]
+
+jobs:
+  claude-review:
+    # Auto-run on PR open. Push-to-PR (synchronize) and reopen do NOT auto-run —
+    # ask for a fresh review by commenting `@claude` on the PR.
+    if: >
+      github.event_name == 'pull_request' ||
+      (github.event_name == 'issue_comment' && github.event.issue.pull_request != null && contains(github.event.comment.body, '@claude')) ||
+      (github.event_name == 'pull_request_review_comment' && contains(github.event.comment.body, '@claude')) ||
+      (github.event_name == 'pull_request_review' && contains(github.event.review.body, '@claude'))
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    permissions:
+      contents: write
+      pull-requests: write
+      issues: write
+      id-token: write
+    steps:
+      - name: Resolve PR head ref
+        id: pr
+        env:
+          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
+        run: |
+          PR=${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.event.issue.number }}
+          REF=$(gh pr view "$PR" --repo ${{ github.repository }} --json headRefName -q .headRefName)
+          echo "ref=$REF" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
+
+      - name: Checkout PR branch
+        uses: actions/checkout@v5
+        with:
+          ref: ${{ steps.pr.outputs.ref }}
+          fetch-depth: 1
+
+      - name: Configure git identity
+        run: |
+          git config user.name "claude-code[bot]"
+          git config user.email "claude-code[bot]@users.noreply.github.com"
+
+      - name: Run Claude Code Review
+        uses: anthropics/claude-code-action@v1
+        with:
+          anthropic_api_key: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}
+          track_progress: true
+          claude_args: |
+            --allowedTools "Bash(gh pr diff:*),Bash(gh pr view:*),Bash(gh pr comment:*),Bash(gh pr checks:*),Bash(gh run view:*),Bash(gh run list:*),Bash(git add:*),Bash(git commit:*),Bash(git push:*),Bash(git diff:*),Bash(git status:*),Bash(git log:*),Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,WebFetch"
+          prompt: |
+            REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
+            PR NUMBER: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number || github.event.issue.number }}
+            PR BRANCH: ${{ steps.pr.outputs.ref }}
+
+            You operate in one of two modes based on the comment / PR body:
+
+            **APPLY mode** — if the request asks for a change ("fix", "rename", "add", "update", etc.):
+            1. Make the edits with Edit/Write.
+            2. `git add` the changed files, `git commit` with a concise message describing the change, and `git push` to the PR branch.
+            3. Post a brief PR comment summarising what was committed.
+            Do not reformat files as part of an APPLY — pre-commit (black/isort/ruff/autoflake)
+            handles formatting on the contributor side.
+
+            **REVIEW mode** — default for `pull_request` events and unclear requests:
+            Review the PR. Use `gh pr diff` to see the changed files.
+
+            **Output shape — read carefully, the reviewer values concision:**
+
+            1. **Always start with a short summary** of what the PR does in your own words. Aim for 1-2 sentences; up to 3-4 only if the PR genuinely changes several independent things. It must read as a *summary*, not a re-explanation of the diff. Plain prose, no bullet.
+            2. **Then either:**
+               - If nothing substantive needs attention: `**LGTM.**` on its own line. Stop.
+               - Otherwise: a flat bullet list of issues. No section headers, no "Substantive / Minor / Looks good" split, no positive recap of what the PR did well.
+            3. **Only raise issues that would change the merge decision or cause a real bug.** No hard count cap — write as many as the PR genuinely needs, but err on the side of fewer. If you're tempted to soften a bullet with "minor", "nit", "cosmetic", or "consider", drop it.
+
+            **What to flag:**
+            - Logic bugs in the diff (off-by-one, wrong branch, missing await, race conditions).
+            - Resource leaks: GPU memory, file handles, subprocess teardown (especially around ray / sglang lifecycles).
+            - Hardcoded secrets / credentials / tokens, or hardcoded absolute paths under `/root/`, `/home/`, etc.
+            - PR description claims that don't match the diff (e.g. "fixes X under `set -e`" when the script doesn't have `set -e`).
+            - **`mpu.get_*` ban**: direct calls to `mpu.get_*` are banned by pre-commit. Use the `ParallelState` wrapper. Exempt: `backends/megatron_utils/parallel.py`, `tools/convert_to_hf.py`, `miles_plugins/`, `examples/`.
+            - **Slurm launcher invariants** (`scripts/slurm/`): if the PR touches `launch_miles.sbatch`, `submit.sh`, or `lib/*.sh`, cross-check `scripts/slurm/docs/launcher.md` (step memory cap, two-phase cleanup, healthcheck). Don't accept changes that break these without explicit justification.
+            - **Tests & docs — only when genuinely necessary.** The bar is high: flag only if the PR introduces non-trivial new behavior in `miles/` that is reasonably testable and has no test, or makes a user-visible change that contradicts existing `docs/` or `README.md`. Do NOT ask for tests on: bug fixes, one-line changes, refactors with no behavior change, scripts under `scripts/`, infra/launcher changes, doc-only PRs. Do NOT ask for doc updates unless the existing doc is now wrong. When in doubt, skip it.
+
+            **Do not flag:**
+            - Missing error handlers for impossible internal states.
+            - Code that "could be more defensive" but is correct as written.
+            - Inconsistent quoting / formatting / naming if the change is internally consistent.
+            - Style / cosmetic / UTF-8-vs-byte / log-noise issues that don't cause incorrect behavior.
+            - "Consider also doing X" expansions of scope beyond what the PR set out to do.
+
+            If you use a `suggestion` block, it must compile and must fix something concrete — not just reword.
+          show_full_output: true
